 "description": "The cypress of the Monterey headlands, a pyramid when young and later a broad, flat-topped, wind-carved crown on a few massive twisting limbs. Its dense foliage of tiny scale leaves is a dark, slightly bronze green and smells of lemon when crushed.",
 "season_notes": {
  "winter": "Storm winds off the sea shear the crown flat and lean it away from the water.",
  "spring": "Fresh tips lighten the dark foliage and yellow pollen sheds.",
  "summer": "Fog wraps the headland trees and beads on the scale leaves.",
  "fall": "Round woody cones the size of walnuts hang closed in clusters, some opening in warm spells."
